<Base title="Counterfactual Extra Pass Simulation | Bence Supola"><div class="container"><div class="case-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Case study 03 · Simulation / Decision modeling</div><h1>Modeling Decisions That Were Never Made</h1><p class="lead">Estimating the value of unrealized extra-pass opportunities through geometry, statistical models and counterfactual simulation.</p><div class="skills"><span class="skill">Simulation</span><span class="skill">Statistical modeling</span><span class="skill">Expected points</span><span class="skill">Decision analytics</span></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>The challenge</h2><p>Conventional box scores describe what happened. They rarely quantify the value of a pass that could have happened instead of a shot. This project asked how an alternative passing decision might change the expected value of a possession.</p><h2>The approach</h2><p>The research combined spatial constraints, modeled passing time, defensive responses and shot-value estimation to evaluate feasible extra-pass scenarios. The simulation was designed to make the assumptions behind these counterfactual comparisons explicit.</p></div><figure class="figure"><img src="/images/extra-pass-duration.png" alt="Side-by-side scatterplots of pass duration in milliseconds versus distance in feet for air and dribble passes, with orange quadratic trend curves." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Unpublished exploratory plots: air-pass and dribble-pass duration versus pass distance. Orange curves show quadratic fits; dispersion illustrates why distance alone cannot precisely predict individual pass durations.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>Modeling pass duration</h2><p>The exploratory scatterplots compare air passes and dribble passes. The quadratic trend curves describe the overall relationship with distance, while the wide spread motivates modeling faster plausible pass durations with quantile regression in the counterfactual workflow.</p><h2>Why this matters</h2><p>The contribution is not merely an additional basketball metric. It is an example of modeling unobserved alternatives: defining feasible choices, estimating their outcomes and evaluating the model rather than assuming simulated outcomes are facts.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.2478/IJCSS-2023-0002">Read the peer-reviewed paper ↗</a></p><p><a href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a></p></div></div></Base>
